{"kind":"report","text":"CHANGES-REQUESTED at exact clean head a668051e39ce7060ef96cfb02d83b2a20c701b0d, artifact ca38797294c5ac307b85b45b40c87e20834032dc.\n\nThe two core moral corrections are right: a ratified completion must not become cancelled merely because its request is retired later, and an authorized third party retiring another actor's promise must not be called that actor's reneging. I also agree with the documented decision not to clear WaitingOn from a stale promise: staleness qualifies the live obligation; it does not discharge it. Do not implement that obsolete condition.\n\nBlocker 1 — fold compatibility is unversioned. This head changes deterministic Workroom fold output, adds a projected status, and intentionally changes the pinned legacy projection, but internal/workroom.ProfileVersion remains workroom-fold@2. A resident may therefore accept an @2 checkpoint produced by the old fold and continue under the new executable, yielding projection results dependent on checkpoint history. Bump the fold profile and pin the invalidation. Hugh has also directed that future profile bumps include honest rebuild progress, so land the pending resident-rebuild progress work before this head or incorporate it and then review the combined exact head.\n\nBlocker 2 — one `revoked` status conflates terminal and nonterminal shapes. Third-party retirement of the request ends the work. Third-party retirement of only a promise leaves the request live and able to receive a replacement promise. This head puts both under terminal[\"revoked\"], so the latter disappears from bounded status and actor work even though the request itself remains live. That is less visible than today's `reneged` attention row. Give request revocation and promise revocation distinct lifecycle treatment (two labels, or an equally explicit discriminator), and test the actor/status lanes for both, not only the fold word.\n\nBlocker 3 — the public contract documentation still describes the old fold. docs/concepts/work-loop.md says the fold emits eight statuses and says ratifier retirement produces withdrawn/cancelled/reneged; docs/reference/gs/status.md likewise describes the old terminal/attention partition. Update the canonical work-loop account and every affected status surface on the same head, with front-matter bases anchored to the new behavior artifact as repository policy requires.\n\nBlocker 4 — the served UI is not in the exact head. `make ui-check` fails after a successful TypeScript/Vite build because internal/service/uidist still contains the predecessor bundle. The resident would serve UI code without the new status. Regenerate and commit the bundle, then make ui-check pass.\n\nArchitecture: affected layers are the Workroom application interpreter/fold, projection/query contract, MCP surface, and browser UI. The semantic-free kernel boundary is preserved and the architecture page already describes these compatibility axes, so its content need not change; the exact fold marker and coupled surfaces do. Security: retirement attribution comes only from effective signed supersessions and does not weaken kernel or authority checks; no security blocker found. Simplification: retirementActors stores actor slices although the only consumer asks a boolean. A single map[event]bool computed by comparing each live superseder with the target author would express the same distinction more directly; distinguish request versus promise in projectCommitments where that lifecycle context is already known.\n\nVerification: make test passed; make vet passed; npm test --prefix ui passed (72 tests); git diff --check passed. make ui-check failed on the uncommitted resident bundle. The review-generated bundle was removed and the worktree is clean again.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:ca38797294c5ac307b85b45b40c87e20834032dc","head":"a668051e39ce7060ef96cfb02d83b2a20c701b0d","verdict":"changes-requested"}}